Wait, but I need to show it's in (s \ t) \ u. That means x is in s \ t and not in u. So from x being in s and not in t, that's s \ t, and from x not in u, that's the \ u part. 

So, putting that together, if x is in s \ (t ∪ u), then x is in s \ t and also in \ u. So, x is in (s \ t) \ u. 

I think that's the right approach. So, in terms of proof steps, take an arbitrary x in s \ (t ∪ u), show it's in s \ t and in \ u, hence in (s \ t) \ u. 

Let me also think about edge cases or examples. Suppose s = {1,2,3}, t = {1}, u = {2}. Then s \ (t ∪ u) is {3}. (s \ t) is {2,3}, then (s \ t) \ u is {3}, so it works. 

Another example: s = {a,b,c}, t = {a}, u = {c}. Then s \ (t ∪ u) is {b}. (s \ t) is {b,c}, then (s \ t) \ u is {b}, so that also works. 

And if u is empty, then s \ (t ∪ u) is s \ t, and (s \ t) \ u is also s \ t, so equality holds in that case. 

