",,True,-1,"Normal practice to show t6 : s \ t ∪ t = s ∪ t is to show that s \ t ∪ t is a subset of s ∪ t, and that s ∪ t is a subset of s \ t ∪ t.
The first is straightforward, because each part of the union is a subset of s ∪ t.
The second is not straightforward because some elements of s are removed by t in the left-hand set.
But we can just show that anything in s ∪ t is in s \ t ∪ t.
Assume x is in s ∪ t, then x is in s or in t.
If x is in s but not in t, then x is in s \ t.
Else, x is in t. So in all cases, x is in s \ t ∪ t.
Which establishes that s ∪ t is a subset of s \ t ∪ t.
