",,True,-1,"While I was trying to solve this problem, I chopped down the theorem to its essence: showing that the set of all elements of the form x * h * x⁻¹ forms a subgroup of G. To do that, I used the SubgroupTests, verifying that the set is nonempty, closed under multiplication, and closed under inversion. I leveraged the properties of subgroups to make some of these steps more straightforward, like using the fact that the identity is in H for inv_mem'. Instead of defining a map and proving surjectivity, I directly checked that all necessary group operation properties hold within the subset. This approach is concise and directly addresses each of the three subgroup criteria.

