",,True,-1,"Let an arbitrary filter t on Set ℕ is a system of sets with the following properties (to be a base of t for filter t lacks proving filter t is a filter on ℕ):

1. All elements of t are sets of ℕ; this is easy, as all items in t2 are of the form Set.univ, Set.₀, SetFinance, and closed under in addition Set.mem, Set.mem, Set.add, etc.

2. If U and V are in t, then U ∩ V is in t; in t2 U ∩ V is Set.mem-(Set.₀, Set.₀), Set.mem-(Set.₀, Set.₀), or Set.₀.

3. If U is in t and U is subset of V, then V is in t; in t2 this is Set.₀ → Set.₀, Set.₀ → Set.₀, and Set.₀ → Set.₀.

Since all three conditions are met, t2 is a filter on ℕ.

